# Rank 1282 / CVE-2022-29930: the Kotlin/Native (`posix`) implementation of
# `sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray` in ktor-utils computed the digest of an
# empty message regardless of its argument, so every caller on that target
# got the same, input-independent SHA-1 output (CWE-330/CWE-342).
#
# The fixing diff is exactly one line in
# ktor-utils/posix/src/io/ktor/util/CryptoNative.kt:
#   -public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest()
#   +public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest(bytes)
# plus a regression test (HashFunctionTest.kt) asserting a fixed input hashes
# to a fixed, non-empty-input digest. NVD's own description ("SHA1
# implementation in JetBrains Ktor Native 2.0.0 returns the same value") and
# PR ktorio/ktor#2966 both match this diff exactly.
#
# The bug is a Kotlin overload-resolution footgun, not a usage mistake:
# `HashFunction` declares a member `fun digest(): ByteArray` (finalize using
# whatever is fed via `update`), and a same-named *extension*
# `fun HashFunction.digest(input: ByteArray, ...): ByteArray` calls
# `update(input); return digest()` as sugar for the common case. Calling
# `Sha1().digest()` with no arguments binds to the member overload -- which
# is entirely legitimate when `update()` is called earlier -- not
# the extension; here it is the *only* call, so `update()` never runs and
# the digest is always of the empty message. `Sha1().digest(bytes)` binds to
# the extension and is correct. The real-world exposure is
# ktor-http/common/src/io/ktor/http/websocket/Utils.kt's
# `websocketServerAccept(nonce)`, which computes
# `sha1(nonce + magic).encodeBase64()` for the `Sec-WebSocket-Accept`
# header -- on the broken Native target this becomes a constant value no
# matter what nonce the client sent, defeating the handshake's anti-replay
# purpose. That call site is untouched at both revisions and reads as entirely
# correct code; the defect is invisible there and lives only in the
# posix-target `sha1()` definition itself.
#
# ATTENTION, no taint-flow shape and no vocabulary that would not be a
# repro-shaped patch. `vyql match` on both revisions below labels nothing at
# all (no source, sink, or control), and `vyql resolve` resolves the
# `Sha1.digest` call, so the miss is vocabulary, not resolution. A scoped
# scan of the ktor-utils tree reports the identical five findings at both
# revisions (3 runs each, `--cache off`): one VYQL-CRY-001 on the *JVM*
# twin CryptoJvm.kt:33 -- the algorithm-choice concept on
# `MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA1")`, a construct the fix never touches --
# plus four VYQL-CRY-003 on unrelated test files; nothing names
# CryptoNative.kt, and ktor-http/common/src (the websocketServerAccept
# caller) scans zero.
#
# The defect is a typestate/precondition fact -- "is `update()` called on
# this receiver ahead of this `digest()` call" -- and equivalently a
# dead-parameter fact: in the NIR graph the vulnerable form's
# `sha1#param#bytes` node carries no outgoing flow edge (the return is fed
# by `Call path=Sha1.digest` alone), while the patched form gains
# `sha1#param#bytes --FLOWS--> Arg` into that same call. No binding
# predicate expresses a parameter that flows nowhere, and no concept covers
# a finalize-without-feed or unused-input classification: code.WeakHash
# (CWE_327) is about choosing a *broken algorithm* (MD5/SHA1/DES/RC4/ECB) --
# SHA-1 is the RFC 6455-mandated, entirely correct choice for a WebSocket
# accept-key, so that concept does not describe this bug even thematically,
# and there is no unused-parameter concept anywhere in the ontology to
# reuse. A bare zero-argument `.digest()` binding measures 1 true to 7 false
# inside ktor itself -- the other seven no-arg `digest()` sites are the
# idiomatic update()-then-digest() finalization (`digest.update(buffer)`
# then `digest.digest()`, `digester.reset(); digester.update(...);
# digester.digest()`, ...), the same two-call pattern as Java's
# `MessageDigest.update()...digest()` -- and the only narrowing that
# separates them (`callee.path == "Sha1.digest"`) names ktor's internal,
# undocumented `Sha1` class, i.e. this incident's own shape. No binding,
# concept, or rule added.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-no-taint-shape, both revisions reject
# VYQL-CRY-003 (weak randomness) and reject_review code.WeakHash (nearest
# thematic concept, still the wrong one) since neither fires on either form.

test "rank1282: Kotlin/Native sha1() calls the no-arg digest(), ignoring its own input"
  lang kotlin
  file CryptoNative.kt
  reject VYQL-CRY-003
  reject_review code.WeakHash
  code
  ```
  package io.ktor.util

  internal interface HashFunction {
      fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size)
      fun digest(): ByteArray
  }

  internal fun HashFunction.digest(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size): ByteArray {
      update(input, offset, length)
      return digest()
  }

  internal class Sha1 : HashFunction {
      override fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int, length: Int) {
          // records the message bytes into internal digest state
      }

      override fun digest(): ByteArray {
          return ByteArray(20)
      }
  }

  /**
   * Compute SHA-1 hash for the specified [bytes]
   */
  public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest()
  ```

test "rank1282: Kotlin/Native sha1() forwards bytes to the extension digest(input)"
  lang kotlin
  file CryptoNative.kt
  reject VYQL-CRY-003
  reject_review code.WeakHash
  code
  ```
  package io.ktor.util

  internal interface HashFunction {
      fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size)
      fun digest(): ByteArray
  }

  internal fun HashFunction.digest(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size): ByteArray {
      update(input, offset, length)
      return digest()
  }

  internal class Sha1 : HashFunction {
      override fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int, length: Int) {
          // records the message bytes into internal digest state
      }

      override fun digest(): ByteArray {
          return ByteArray(20)
      }
  }

  /**
   * Compute SHA-1 hash for the specified [bytes]
   */
  public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest(bytes)
  ```
